{"name":"Pixel Bleeding","hex":"#bb0022","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pixel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the tiny dots that make up the representation of an image in a computer's memory.","One of the squares that make up a work of pixel art or a zoomed-in image in a computer."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To create computer graphics by editing individual pixels."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pixel"},{"term":"bleeding","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of bleed"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Losing blood.","extreme, outright; bloody, blasted."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bleeding"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pixel","blocks":[{"text":"In digital imaging, a pixel, pel, or picture element is the smallest addressable element of a raster image. In a display device, dot matrix printer, or ASCII art a pixel is the smallest element whose output can generally be controlled independently."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Pixel-example.png/330px-Pixel-example.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pixel","wikidata":"Q355198"},{"title":"Bleeding","blocks":[{"text":"Bleeding, hemorrhage, haemorrhage or blood loss, is blood escaping from the circulatory system from damaged blood vessels. Bleeding can occur internally, or externally either through a natural opening such as the mouth, nose, ear, urethra, genitalia or anus, or through a puncture in the skin.\nHypovolemia is a massive decrease in blood volume, and death by excessive loss of blood is referred to as exsanguination. Typically, a healthy person can endure a loss of 10–15% of the total blood volume without serious medical difficulties. The stopping or controlling of bleeding is called hemostasis and is an important part of both first aid and surgery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Bleeding_finger.jpg/330px-Bleeding_finger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bleeding","wikidata":"Q166019"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Closeup_of_pixels.JPG/330px-Closeup_of_pixels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Closeup_of_pixels.JPG/330px-Closeup_of_pixels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A photograph of subpixel display elements on a laptop's LCD screen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACloseup_of_pixels.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/ReconstructionsFromPixels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/ReconstructionsFromPixels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A pixel does not need to be rendered as a small square. This image shows alternative ways of reconstructing an image from a set of pixel values, using dots, lines, or smooth filtering.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReconstructionsFromPixels.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Linguagem_Eletr%C3%B4nica.jpg/500px-FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Linguagem_Eletr%C3%B4nica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Linguagem_Eletr%C3%B4nica.jpg/500px-FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Lingu